<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vidyaranya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1386</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vidyaranya was a fourteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> scholar and ascetic who became head of the Sringeri monastery founded by Adi Shankara. Later tradition credits him with guiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harihara-i">Harihara I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukka-raya-i">Bukka I</a> in founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, though many historians doubt the story. Works on Advaita <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a>, notably the Panchadasi, are attributed to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vidyaranya.jpg" alt="Vidyaranya" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This plate is contained in the book “Vijayanagara Paintings” · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate-5-vidyaranya-taken-in-procession.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
